  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a\nmean amplified trough of low pressure across the West with a \nlow centered over the Pacific Northwest and its associated \ntrough axis extending SSW to just off the southern California \nCoast, and a strong ridge of high pressure in the Eastern \nPacific near 33N/147W. The latest GOES precipitable water \nimagery indicated modest moisture (PWATs 1.00-1.20 inches) still\nresiding across SW AZ this morning, with lower PWATs between \n0.60-0.80 inches from Tucson east.\n\nWith the trough axis west of our neck of the woods, pressure\ngradients are still tight enough to result in breezy southwest\nwinds 15-25 mph with gusts to 35 mph across the majority of\nSoutheast Arizona today. The trough axis is progged to finally\ndrag east through SE AZ this evening and into Friday. Weak\ndynamics along with the aforementioned moisture in place will \nresult in scattered mainly light showers as it passes through. \nNothing major or impactful, generally less than a tenth inch \nwith some spots getting upwards of a quarter-third inch.\n\nThe more impactful weather for Southeast Arizona will be late\nthis week into this weekend as a strong surface high pressure\narea backs into the area from the E/NE resulting in strong to \nvery strong ELY/SELY winds. This feature evolves from an\nanomalously strong ridge of high pressure aloft currently in \nthe Eastern Pacific that tracks west the next few days and then \nflops into the Great Basin and northern Intermountain West and \nintensifies the second half of the week. Both the 12/00Z GEFS \nand ECMWF ensemble members indicate a strong surface high \ndeveloping along the Intermountain West between 2.5 and 3.5 \nstandard deviations from normal, and driving this feature SSW/S \nalong the Rockies into SE AZ as early as Thursday night. \n\nForecast models indicate a very strong pressure gradient across\nSE AZ, with the 12/00Z deterministic GFS showing 850mb winds as\nhigh as 45-55 kts across our CWA between 16/06-18Z Friday. \nStrong stability around and just above the ridgetops during this\ntime period will potentially make this more of a mountain-wave \nevent with very strong winds hugging the lee side of the higher\nterrain like the far eastern portions of the Tucson Metro Area \n(lee side of the Rincon mountains) and the far NW side of town \nbetween Oro Valley-Catalina (lee side of the Catalina mountains).\nThis is contrasted to the more typical mountain-gap events we \nsee where the strongest winds are in the ELY/SELY aligned \nvalleys/terrain gaps.\n\nAlthough the 12/00Z EOF patterns (Empirical Orthogonal \nFunction) shows little variance amongst the ensemble members \nwith regards to the strong upper low that develops over the \nDesert Southwest when the ridge builds into the Great \nBasin/Intermountain West, pattern recognition tells me that \nvariance in solutions may indeed be present depending on the \ntiming of the upper ridge building into the area with the \ndeparting upper trough in the northern plains and upper midwest.\nSo, it is important to get the word out that we will likely be \nimpacted with breezy to windy E/SE winds Friday and Saturday, \nbut the intensity is still in question this far out. We will \ncontinue to closely monitor future runs.",
